)"] [:db/cas 17592186416429 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416435 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form.\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=-5 \\\\  & y=3 \\\\  & z=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=-5$, $y=3$, and $z=2.$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form.\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=-5 \\\\  & y=3 \\\\  & z=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=-5$, $y=3$, and $z=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186416436 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416437 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416438 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 24 & -36 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 24 & -36 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+6y-9z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-6y+9z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-6y+9z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 24 & -36 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 24 & -36 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+6y-9z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-6y+9z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-6y+9z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416481 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n- **II.** The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- **III.** Any $5\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n- **II.** The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ has one negative singular value.\n- **III.** Any $5\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values."] [:db/cas 17592186416482 :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]?$" "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186416482 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}17-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (17-\\lambda )(17-\\lambda )-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +289-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +225 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -25) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}17-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (17-\\lambda )(17-\\lambda )-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +289-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +225 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -25) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416483 :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186416483 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416484 :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186416484 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda -5\\l