ligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417167 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417168 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417169 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417170 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417171 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417172 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417173 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417184 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417184 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417185 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417185 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417186 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417186 :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417187 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417187 :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-37 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-37 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417188 :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}},\\,C={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417188 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417189 :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}},\\,C={\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417189 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -9 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -9 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417190 :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3}.$" "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417190 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that since $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417191 :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}},\\,C={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417191 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 16 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 13 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 16 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 13 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}13 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}13 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417192 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417192 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 6-7\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2},$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 6-7\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2},$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417194 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186417198 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186417199 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186417202 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only" "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186417204 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\text{ }\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417211 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y+1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+y+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+(-x-1)+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\text{ }0,\\text{ }1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=-0-1=-1.$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get $y=-1-1=-2.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get $y=-(-1)-1=0.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y+1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+y+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+(-x-1)+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\text{ }0,\\text{ }1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=-0-1=-1.$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get $y=-1-1=-2.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get $y=-(-1)-1=0.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\text{and}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417219 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $4x=2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =2y \\\\ 4(0) & =2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ 2a+2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ 2(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(2a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ 2(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 2x\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $4x=2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =2y \\\\ 4(0) & =2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 2b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 2b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ 2a+2b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ 2(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(2a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ 2(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186417356 :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ u+2{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where\n$[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ u+2{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417356 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+2{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =4v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6u & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 4v-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =24uv-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=24(1)(1)-2=22.\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+2{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =4v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}6u & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 4v-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =24uv-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=24(1)(1)-2=22.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417362 :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417363 :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417364 :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" "{D}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417365 :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 3\\end{aligned}]" "{D}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417367 :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417369 :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417370 :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" "{D}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417371 :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]" "{D}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417373 :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]" "{D}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417374 :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417378 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186417378 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186417379 :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $D$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${D}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $D$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${D}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417379 :question/worked-solution "We write down $D$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $D$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${D}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We write down $D$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $D$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${D}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{D}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417712 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" "{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] [:db/cas 17592186417718 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" "{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] [:db/cas 17592186417720 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ 