\\\ k \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3+k+2 \\\\ 4+2k+6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}k+5=0 \\\\ 2k+10=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-5.$" "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ k \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3+k+2 \\\\ 4+2k+6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}k+5=0 \\\\ 2k+10=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417739 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417739 :question/worked-solution "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, none of the vectors lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$" "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, none of the vectors lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186417740 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186417740 :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have one free variable, ${x}_{2}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2},$ so the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}.\n$$" "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have one free variable, ${x}_{2}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2},$ so the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417741 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -12\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -12\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186417741 :question/worked-solution "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2k-6 \\\\ -k+3 \\\\ 4k-12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2k-6=0 \\\\ -k+3=0 \\\\ 4k-12=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=3.$" "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2k-6 \\\\ -k+3 \\\\ 4k-12\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2k-6=0 \\\\ -k+3=0 \\\\ 4k-12=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417742 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186417742 :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Null}(A)={0}.$" "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Null}(A)={0}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417836 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+v-1 \\\\ -u-3v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+v \\\\ -u-3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the image of $P$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a column vector $u$ containing the coordinates of $P:$ \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nApplying $T$ to $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of ${P}^{′}$ are $(-12,1).$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+v-1 \\\\ -u-3v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+v \\\\ -u-3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the image of $P$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a column vector $u$ containing the coordinates of $P:$ \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nApplying $T$ to $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of ${P}^{′}$ are $(-12,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186417837 :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?"] [:db/cas 17592186417837 :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-2|=2.\n$$" "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-2|=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417839 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417841 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417842 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417843 :answer/value "z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "z=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417844 :answer/value "z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "z=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417845 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}50 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 75\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}50 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 75\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417846 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417847 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417851 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417852 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417853 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417854 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417855 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417856 :answer/value "z=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417857 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417858 :answer/value "z=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417859 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ \\frac{15}{7}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ \\frac{15}{7}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417860 :answer/value "z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "z=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417861 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417862 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417869 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417869 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-60\\,}{\\,\\,\\,30\\,}b \\\\  & =-2b \\\\  & =-2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-60\\,}{\\,\\,\\,30\\,}b \\\\  & =-2b \\\\  & =-2\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417870 :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the distance between $u$ and $\\text{Span}{w}.$" "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the distance between $u$ and $\\text{Span}{w}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417870 :question/worked-solution "The distance between the vector $u$ and the vector space $\\text{Span}{w}$ is given by $‖u-\\overset{^}{u}‖$, where $\\overset{^}{u}$ in the orthogonal projection of $u$ onto $\\text{Span}{w}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $u$ onto $\\text{Span}{w}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,u & =\\frac{w\\cdot u}{w\\cdot w}w \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 6}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}w \\\\  & =\\frac{8+2+4+6}{4+1+4+1}w \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{10}w \\\\  & =2w \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u-\\overset{^}{u}‖ & =\\sqrt{({u}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{u}}_{1})^{2}+({u}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{u}}_{2})^{2}+({u}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{u}}_{3})^{2}+({u}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{u}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4-4)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(-2-(-4))^{2}+(6-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+0+4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The distance between the vector $u$ and the vector space $\\text{Span}{w}$ is given by $‖u-\\hat{u}‖$, where $\\hat{u}$ in the orthogonal projection of $u$ onto $\\text{Span}{w}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $u$ onto $\\text{Span}{w}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,u & =\\frac{w\\cdot u}{w\\cdot w}w \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 6}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}w \\\\  & =\\frac{8+2+4+6}{4+1+4+1}w \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{10}w \\\\  & =2w \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u-\\hat{u}‖ & =\\sqrt{({u}_{1}-{\\hat{u}}_{1})^{2}+({u}_{2}-{\\hat{u}}_{2})^{2}+({u}_{3}-{\\hat{u}}_{3})^{2}+({u}_{4}-{\\hat{u}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4-4)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(-2-(-4))^{2}+(6-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+0+4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417871 :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ If $x=y+z,$ where $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ then $z=$" "Let $x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $x=y+z,$ where $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ then $z=$"] [:db/cas 17592186417871 :question/worked-solution "![](images/d8/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{a\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 7+(-3)(-6)+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{6+21+18+1}{4+9+9+1}a \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{23}a \\\\  & =2a \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z & =x-{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ as shown in the picture above.\n\nIn conclusion, $z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" "![](images/d8/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{a\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 7+(-3)(-6)+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{6+21+18+1}{4+9+9+1}a \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{23}a \\\\  & =2a \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ z & =x-{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ as shown in the picture above.\n\nIn conclusion, $z=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417872 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -13 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -13 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417872 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & 